      "text": "We start by considering the given equation of the parabola: \\( x = 2y^2 - 6y + 3 \\). To find the \\(x\\)-intercept, we set \\( y = 0 \\) and solve for \\( x \\):\n\n\\[\nx = 2(0)^2 - 6(0) + 3 = 3\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the \\(x\\)-intercept is \\((3, 0)\\), so \\(a = 3\\).\n\nNext, to find the \\(y\\)-intercepts, we set \\( x = 0 \\) and solve for \\( y \\):\n\n\\[\n0 = 2y^2 - 6y + 3\n\\]\n\nThis is a quadratic equation in \\( y \\). We solve it using the quadratic formula \\( y = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a} \\), where \\( a = 2 \\), \\( b = -6 \\), and \\( c = 3 \\):\n\n\\[\ny = \\frac{-(-6) \\pm \\sqrt{(-6)^2 - 4 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 3}}{2 \\cdot 2} = \\frac{6 \\pm \\sqrt{36 - 24}}{4} = \\frac{6 \\pm \\sqrt{12}}{4} = \\frac{6 \\pm 2\\sqrt{3}}{4} = \\frac{3 \\pm \\sqrt{3}}{2}\n\\]\n\nThus, the \\(y\\)-intercepts are \\( \\left(0, \\frac{3 + \\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) \\) and \\( \\left(0, \\frac{3 - \\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) \\), so \\( b = \\frac{3 + \\sqrt{3}}{2} \\) and \\( c = \\frac{3 - \\sqrt{3}}{2} \\).\n\nFinally, we find the sum \\( a + b + c \\):\n\n\\[\na + b + c = 3 + \\frac{3 + \\sqrt{3}}{2} + \\frac{3 - \\sqrt{3}}{2}\n\\]\n\nCombining the terms, we get:\n\n\\[\na + b + c = 3 + \\frac{3 + \\sqrt{3} + 3 - \\sqrt{3}}{2} = 3 + \\frac{6}{2} = 3 + 3 = 6\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{6}\n\\]",
